WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:06.780
Now that we know how to import our character and animations, let's jump in and take a look at our

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.660
character in the engine and check out the different animation subeditors to see how we can

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.580
preview our animations and get a basic understanding of how to navigate through

4
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.539
the different animation editors and what you can do within each one.

5
00:00:20.989 --> 00:00:24.589
To preview our character in the engine we want to open up the skeletal mesh

6
00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:29.710
and you can see that's one of these pink icons just here and you can tell that just because

7
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.990
it says skeletal mesh at the bottom of the icon or if you hover over it

8
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.189
it'll also tell you it's a skeletal mesh just in brackets at the top there.

9
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:41.969
So if we double click this, this opens up our skeletal mesh editor.

10
00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:50.189
Now this is where we can preview our skeleton, our character which has the skeleton here

11
00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:52.590
and it has the mesh that we have skinned to it.

12
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.149
Now this is also where you would assign the materials for your character.

13
00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:00.829
So just down here in the asset details you can assign any materials that you might have

14
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.870
as well as LOD information that you might need to set up for your character just below.

15
00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:10.659
You can also see you've got access to your skeleton tree here so you can see all of the

16
00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:14.099
bones for your characters or any sockets that you might have set up to attach things

17
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:16.019
to your characters you'd find in here as well.

18
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.689
And another useful window here is the morph target preview window.

19
00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:24.370
So this allows you to preview any morph targets that you've got set up for your character.

20
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.969
So if you set up any blend shapes in an external software and brought them into the engine

21
00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:33.170
this is where you'd be able to preview them and turn them on and off and see

22
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.010
whether or not they're working correctly.

23
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.150
And along the top right of the toolbar here you'll see we've got a few different icons.

24
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.109
Now these give you access to the other animation sub editor windows.

25
00:01:46.109 --> 00:01:50.269
So whenever you're in one of these windows you will always have these icons at the top

26
00:01:50.269 --> 00:01:52.510
to give you access to the other windows.

27
00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:56.189
So we can jump through to the skeleton, we can see any animations,

28
00:01:56.989 --> 00:02:00.909
any animation blueprints or physics assets that are related to this

29
00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:02.269
skeletal mesh that we have open.

30
00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:09.340
So if we jump into the skeleton here this is where you would access and edit your skeleton.

31
00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:13.389
So on the left hand side here you can see all the bones that are in your skeleton.

32
00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:18.030
You can select any of these and you can manipulate them and just see how your message

33
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:18.990
mesh is deforming.

34
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.139
You could also attach or create sockets should I say for your skeleton which is what you'd

35
00:02:26.139 --> 00:02:29.259
use to attach items to your character.

36
00:02:29.259 --> 00:02:32.860
Whether that's at runtime or through sequences or however you want to do that.

37
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.870
You can also preview attachments inside this window here as well if you have a socket.

38
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.610
Now on the right hand side here you have your preview scene settings tab.

39
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.650
So if you have multiple skeletal meshes that are using the same skeleton

40
00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.650
this is where you could change the preview for those.

41
00:02:50.289 --> 00:02:54.530
So you can see on the right hand side here we've got two different skeletal meshes that

42
00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.810
use this same skeleton.

43
00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.090
Now we can swap between those and see what that preview looks like.

44
00:02:59.789 --> 00:03:04.030
And again you can just manipulate the joints there just to see how that deformation works.

45
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.719
And on the bottom right here we have the asset browser.

46
00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.360
Now this is where you can access animations or blend spaces or montages that are being

47
00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:18.479
used with this skeleton or the skeletal mesh.

48
00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.460
Now if we double click and open up one of these animation sequences here

49
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.520
you can see that it automatically opens up this animation sequence editor window.

50
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.400
And you can see that on the top right here with the the icon that's highlighted.

51
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.680
This is where you would preview your animations.

52
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.000
So inside here again you've got access to your skeleton tree.

53
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.159
You can see all the the joints if you need those.

54
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.560
There's also the asset details tab.

55
00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:51.919
So this is where you could modify or edit any animation related properties.

56
00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:56.000
So you could change the rate scale and make the animation quicker or slow it down.

57
00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:00.400
You could also activate or deactivate root motion.

58
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.080
So if your animation has root motion you can turn that on and off just here.

59
00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.050
And a host of other animation related properties.

60
00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.530
You also have access again to the preview scene settings tab.

61
00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.290
So we could change that to be Quinn again and just see that animation

62
00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.370
is looking with that character.

63
00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.209
If you're perhaps trying to use the same animation with multiple characters

64
00:04:22.209 --> 00:04:26.290
just make sure it looks good and it's working with the multiple characters that you have.

65
00:04:27.149 --> 00:04:29.470
And along the bottom here is the timeline.

66
00:04:30.029 --> 00:04:33.389
So this is obviously where you can scrub through the animation forwards and backwards.

67
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.949
You can add notifies.

68
00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:41.310
So these green icons here which just trigger code essentially to do something at runtime.

69
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.269
You can also access the curves related to this character that might be

70
00:04:46.269 --> 00:04:51.149
blend shape curves or they could be bone tracks that you have set up for your character.

71
00:04:51.149 --> 00:04:53.870
You'll be able to see that in the window just down the bottom here.

72
00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.230
And again you have the asset browser.

73
00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:01.389
So again if you have another animation that you want to jump to you could just double click

74
00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:06.430
open any of those up and also things like blend spaces so you could preview those here.

75
00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:12.350
So if I hit play here we can go from the slow walk to the standard walk and then

76
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:16.100
blend into the run so you can preview all that stuff in here as well.

77
00:05:17.519 --> 00:05:21.279
Now something else you can do which isn't immediately clear if I just jump back to one of

78
00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:28.000
the animations rather than the blend space is you can actually edit this animation in sequencer.

79
00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:33.680
So if you hit these two little arrows at the top here choose editing sequencer

80
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:40.220
and then edit with fk control rig that'll then give you an option to bake this down to

81
00:05:41.339 --> 00:05:43.420
the fk control rig within a sequencer.

82
00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:56.079
So if I click bake to control rig and I go to our level here we can see that as we scrub

83
00:05:56.079 --> 00:05:59.759
through this our animation has been baked down to this control rig here.

84
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.879
So what I could do is grab one of these controls which just represent each bone in the

85
00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:12.129
body and maybe I can just grab a few of these and delete them and add a new key where

86
00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:17.470
he raises his arm and you'll see that as I scrub through this the arm now raises up and

87
00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.420
down and what you can do with that if I just scrub back to the top is right click the

88
00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:26.740
character and bake animation sequence and that just means we can save this as a new

89
00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.540
animation or we could even overwrite the existing animation and it's just a really nice way and

90
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.100
a quick way of making edits to existing animations that you have inside the project.

91
00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:40.660
Now we do have a lot of other courses that go over sequencer and control rigs in much more

92
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.180
detail so be sure to check those out if that's something that you want to learn more about.

93
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.819
Our next animation editor window is the animation blueprint so if we click this icon just

94
00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:56.579
up here here we have the animation blueprint which is essentially a tool which controls the

95
00:06:56.579 --> 00:07:02.579
animations of the skeletal mesh by allowing you to blend animations control bones

96
00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:07.300
and create different logic that essentially defines the final animation pose.

97
00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:12.720
So in here we've got two main graphs we have the event graph and the anim graph.

98
00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:20.540
So if I double click that here's the event graph and this is similar to a regular blueprint

99
00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:25.819
where you can handle different events and variables and logic that would trigger a change

100
00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:31.819
in state of the animation and then you have the anim graph. Now this is where you would define

101
00:07:31.819 --> 00:07:38.220
the final animation output pose so where you can control the blending of the animations

102
00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:43.500
set up state machines and things that basically determine the final animation output pose.

103
00:07:44.509 --> 00:07:50.269
So essentially the event graph takes information like player input and then passes that along

104
00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:55.230
to the anim graph and the anim graph determines what animation should be playing based on that

105
00:07:55.230 --> 00:08:02.459
input. And finally we have the physics editor window just at the top right here. Now this is

106
00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:06.139
where you would create and edit any of the collision bodies that will be used for your

107
00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:10.459
skeletal mesh in relation to any physics that you have going on in your project.

108
00:08:11.339 --> 00:08:16.939
So maybe you want to make your character ragdoll or have things bump into your character you'd

109
00:08:16.939 --> 00:08:21.579
use this window here to edit all of these physics bodies that you can see adding and

110
00:08:21.579 --> 00:08:26.620
deleting from the skeleton tree on the left hand side here and then readjusting and repositioning

111
00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:31.259
in the viewport to make sure they fit your character mesh properly and to ensure that things

112
00:08:31.259 --> 00:08:33.659
react correctly with the mesh.
